// How a crux skill gets Piton fluency. It is abstract, so it never
// compiles or is emitted on its own. PitonSkillBase extends it, so
// every crux skill carries this guidance once, as a section after its
// own prompt. A skill that needs more than this, such as a different
// failure behaviour, says so in its own prompt.
export abstract anchor Fluency:
    pitonFluency:
        Fluency is read once per context. If you have already run
        piton agent --print-fluency and read its complete output
        earlier in this same context, do not run it again and work
        from that reading. Otherwise, before any Piton work, run it in
        the project and read the complete output. Treat it as the
        authoritative reference for Piton syntax and semantics,
        project structure, imports and exports, inheritance and
        composition, compiler and analyzer commands, project
        conventions, and validation. Do not invent Piton syntax,
        commands, or conventions. Reuse the compiler and analyzer
        wherever possible rather than developing a separate
        interpretation of Piton semantics. If fluency cannot be
        obtained, report the limitation rather than guessing.
